speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, in calling this bill today I wonder whether the government House leader is really a masochist or not. Yesterday at the time of adjournment I was discussing the unprecedented delegation of arbitrary power to the executive under this bill. One of the key phrases contained in this bill is, and I quote:
-profit margin ... that is greater than he would customarily obtain on such sale or distribution;
It is to be the cabinet alone that will determine in its own way what this phrase means.

I am sorry; perhaps I used the wrong example when I spoke of a can of tomatoes. I should have used the example of a pair of shoes; the hon. member would have had no problem with that. Your party says you would freeze the price of everything in this country, but you start by excluding agricultural products. You are not very logical in your policy.

Mr. Speaker, had they only read the bill they would perhaps have reached a very simple conclusion, that found in the editorial of the newspaper Le Devoir on April 30, 1974, and I quote;
To those circumstances ...

Mr. Speaker, may I refer the hon. member to section 50(5) which states unequivocably that the natural products of agriculture are not included. The subsection reads in part;
-this part does not apply to an article ... that is owned by a primary producer or fisherman or to any sale or distribution of such an article in an unprocessed state by such a primary producer or fisherman.
Does he not realize that such language makes the bill apply to those products at every further level of trade beyond that?
